From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] h264_mvc: look a picture's pts up inside its own packet

Now and then two neighbouring MVC pictures come out carrying each
other's pts: on one film, 35.827 then 35.786, in about one play in
seven, at the same pair. Kodi checks the pts of each packet in at its
start, in decode order, correctly. The firmware reports where each
displayed picture's data starts; for the B picture of that pair it
reported a position 65 bytes short of its packet (elsewhere within a
few bytes), inside the reference picture's packet before it. The lookup
gave the B picture the reference's pts, and the reference, looked up
after it in display order, took the B picture's.

Look the pts up 256 bytes past the reported position: past the
shortfall seen, and far inside any coded picture, so a position the
firmware gets exactly or a few bytes either way still lands in its own
packet.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
---
--- a/drivers/frame_provider/decoder/h264_mvc/vh264_mvc.c
+++ b/drivers/frame_provider/decoder/h264_mvc/vh264_mvc.c
@@ -404,6 +404,13 @@
 	vfbuf_ref(view, id);
 }
 
+/*
+ * How far into a picture's packet its pts is looked up, in bytes. More than
+ * the firmware's position is seen to fall short (65), far less than any coded
+ * picture (tens of kilobytes).
+ */
+#define MVC_PTS_LOOKUP_INTO 256
+
 /* a free slot, else a STALE one, else -1 */
 static int vf_slot_take(void)
 {
@@ -1248,9 +1255,17 @@
 					pr_info
 					("error case, invalid stream offset\n");
 				}
+				/*
+				 * Looked up a little into the picture's own packet:
+				 * the firmware now and then reports a position some
+				 * tens of bytes before it, inside the previous
+				 * packet, whose pts that picture then takes while the
+				 * previous one, looked up next, takes this one's.
+				 */
 				if (pts_lookup_offset_us64
 					(PTS_TYPE_VIDEO,
-					 vfpool_idx[slot].stream_offset, &pts,
+					 vfpool_idx[slot].stream_offset + MVC_PTS_LOOKUP_INTO,
+					 &pts,
 					 &frame_size,
 					 0x10000, &pts_us64) == 0)
 					pts_valid = 1;
